\title{Digit Sums of Periodic Base-$b$ Expansions of $1/p$:\\ The Full-Reptend and Half-Period Laws}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-01}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a prime, let $b \ge 2$ be an integer with $p \nmid b$, and consider the eventually periodic base-$b$ expansion of $1/p$. The length of its period equals the multiplicative order $\ord_p(b)$. We prove two exact digit-sum identities governing the two most symmetric regimes. First, when $b$ is a primitive root modulo $p$---that is, $\ord_p(b) = p-1$---the digits of one period sum to $(b-1)(p-1)/2$. Second, when $\ord_p(b) = (p-1)/2$ and $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, the digits sum to $(b-1)(p-1)/4$. The proofs rest on a single structural observation: the digit sum is a linear image of the sum of the remainder orbit. All base-dependent complexity is thereby transferred to a purely group-theoretic quantity---the sum of the residues in the cyclic subgroup generated by $b$---which is computed by a triangular-number identity in the full-period case and by a negation-symmetry pairing argument in the half-period case, the latter enabled precisely by $-1$ being a quadratic residue when $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$. We situate both identities as extremal instances of a unified order-indexed digit-sum law, connect them to Midy's theorem, and discuss the average-digit corollary and directions for generalization.
\end{abstract}

